---
/**
 * Eilik vs EMO — the first product-versus-product page on the site.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE AND NOT A CATEGORY COMPARE. Every other /compare/ route holds a
 * whole range side by side. This one holds two machines, because Google's own
 * People Also Ask carries the question in almost these words — "Which is
 * better, Eilik or Emo?" — and a category page answers a different question.
 *
 * A STATIC ROUTE, deliberately. /compare/[category].astro would otherwise try
 * to resolve "eilik-vs-emo" as a category slug and return a 404. Astro gives a
 * static path precedence over a dynamic one, so this file is the answer.
 *
 * THE VERDICT IS NOT A COIN TOSS AND IT IS NOT A SALE. One of these two has a
 * buy button on this site and the other does not, and the page says why in the
 * first screen rather than burying it: the EMO listings on Amazon US are a
 * different brand's, so we route nobody to them. That is the single most useful
 * thing a reader arriving on this question can be told, and it happens to be
 * the thing an affiliate page would be most tempted to leave out.
 *
 * FAQ SCHEMA COMES FROM THE QUESTIONS THIS PAGE VISIBLY ANSWERS. The three
 * below are rendered on the page in the same words. Nothing is emitted that a
 * reader cannot read.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { breadcrumbsFor } from "../../lib/routing";
import FaqList from "../../components/FaqList.astro";
import ArticleFooter from "../../components/ArticleFooter.astro";
import { srcsetFor } from "../../lib/srcset";
import { articleSchema, faqPageSchema, imageObjectSchema, webPageSchema } from "../../lib/seo";
import { FOUNDER } from "../../content/team";
import { MEDIA_ASSETS } from "../../content/media/assets";
import "../../styles/prose.css";

const PATH = "/compare/eilik-vs-emo/";
const crumbs = breadcrumbsFor(PATH);
const LAST_REVIEWED = "2026-08-09";

const HERO = "/media/companion/emo-vs-eilik/hero.webp";
const heroAsset = MEDIA_ASSETS.find((a) => a.src === HERO);

const TITLE = "Eilik vs EMO: which desk robot is actually better?";

const FAQ = [
  {
    q: "Which is better, Eilik or EMO?",
    a:
      "Eilik, for most people, and the reason is availability rather than personality. Both are " +
      "expressive desk robots at a similar price. Eilik can be bought from a listing whose own " +
      "fields say Energize Lab. The EMO listings on Amazon US say EMOPET, not Living.AI, so the " +
      "safer route to a genuine EMO is Living.AI's own store — which is slower and costs more.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is the EMO on Amazon a real Living.AI EMO?",
    a:
      "We could not establish that it is. The listing we were asked to check gives its brand and " +
      "its manufacturer as EMOPET, sells at $419 against Living.AI's own $369 for the same " +
      "configuration, and Living.AI staff say on their own forum that their store is the only " +
      "place they can vouch for. We carry no buy button for EMO on that evidence.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do either of them need a subscription?",
    a:
      "Neither charges one to work. EMO's optional AI features have been sold as an add-on by " +
      "Living.AI at various points; Eilik has never had a subscription of any kind. Anything " +
      "advertising an EMO with a mandatory monthly fee is not selling a Living.AI product.",
  },
];

const pageSchema = [
  webPageSchema({
    path: PATH,
    name: TITLE,
    description:
      "Two expressive desk robots at a similar price. Which one to buy, which one to skip, and why only one of them has a buy button here.",
  }),
  articleSchema({
    headline: TITLE,
    description:
      "Eilik and EMO compared on what they do, what they cost and whether you can buy a genuine one.",
    path: PATH,
    dateModified: LAST_REVIEWED,
    authorName: FOUNDER.name,
    authorPath: FOUNDER.path,
    image: HERO,
  }),
  /* Only the image this page renders, and its real dimensions. */
  ...(heroAsset && heroAsset.width && heroAsset.height
    ? [
        imageObjectSchema({
          path: heroAsset.src,
          caption: heroAsset.altText ?? "",
          width: heroAsset.width,
          height: heroAsset.height,
        }),
      ]
    : []),
  faqPageSchema(FAQ, PATH),
];
---

<Base
  title="Eilik vs EMO — Which Desk Robot Is Better? | BotPlanet"
  description="Eilik and EMO compared on price, personality and whether you can buy a genuine one. Only one of them has a buy button here, and this page says why."
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={pageSchema}
  ogImage={HERO}
  ogType="article"
  narrow
>
  <header class="bp-vs-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Head to head</span>
    <h1 class="bp-vs-head__h1">{TITLE}</h1>
    <p class="bp-vs-head__stand">
      Two small robots that sit on a desk and react to you, within about eighty dollars of each
      other. The specification difference is real but it is not what decides this. What decides it
      is that one of them can be bought with confidence from a mainstream retailer and the other
      cannot.
    </p>
    {heroAsset && (
      <figure class="bp-vs-head__hero">
        <img
          src={HERO}
          srcset={srcsetFor(HERO)}
          sizes="(min-width: 1100px) 1040px, 100vw"
          alt={heroAsset.altText ?? ""}
          width={heroAsset.width}
          height={heroAsset.height}
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </figure>
    )}
  </header>

  <article class="bp-prose">
    <h2 id="the-answer">The short answer</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Buy the Eilik.</strong> Not because it is the better robot on paper — that is closer
      than either side's marketing suggests — but because you can order one and be reasonably sure
      what arrives. Eilik sells on Amazon US under a listing whose own brand field reads Energize
      Lab, which is the company that makes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      EMO is the more capable machine and the harder one to buy safely. Searching for it on Amazon
      US returns EMOPET and a run of similarly named desk robots. On 9 August 2026 we checked the
      specific listing readers keep sending us. Its brand field and its manufacturer field both read
      EMOPET. It asks $419 for what its own model number calls EMO GO HOME, where Living.AI's own
      store sells that configuration for $369 — fifty dollars above the maker, which is the wrong
      direction for an authorised reseller. And Living.AI's staff, posting on Living.AI's own forum,
      describe their store as the only legitimate place to buy one.
    </p>
    <p>
      So: <strong>we carry a buy button for Eilik and none for EMO.</strong> If you want an EMO, buy
      it from living.ai directly and check the box says Living.AI before you accept it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-they-actually-do">What each one actually does</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Eilik</strong> is a pair of robots pretending to be one product. A single unit is
      expressive enough on a desk, but the thing it is built around is two of them reacting to each
      other — they bicker, they high-five, and that is genuinely the pitch. It has arms, which
      nothing else at this price does. Energize Lab sells it in several colourways and alongside a
      DQ variant, an AI Station, a Panxer and an Eiliko, which is worth knowing before you search:
      the range is wider than the product.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>EMO</strong> walks. It has legs, it moves around a desk under its own power, it
      recognises faces and it responds to voice. It is the more advanced machine by a clear margin,
      and it is also roughly three times the price of an Eilik once you have a genuine one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="who-should-buy-neither">Who should buy neither</h2>
    <p>
      If you are buying this for a child under about eight, buy neither. Both are desk objects with
      small parts and neither is built for handling by small hands; a Miko 3 or a Loona is designed
      for that job and survives it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want a robot that does something — vacuums, watches the house, mows — buy neither.
      These are companions. They perform no task and they are not pretending to.
    </p>
    <p>
      And if the appeal is specifically a robot that walks around your desk, an Eilik will
      disappoint you and you should wait until you can buy an EMO from its maker rather than take a
      chance on a listing that will not name Living.AI.
    </p>

    <h2 id="read-the-full-reviews">The full reviews</h2>
    <p>
      Each of these has its own page with the specification, the range traps and the buying advice
      in full: <a href="/robots/companion-robots/eilik/">the Eilik review</a> and
      <a href="/robots/companion-robots/living-ai-emo/">the EMO review</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <FaqList
    id="faq"
    eyebrow="Questions"
    title="Questions people actually ask"
    intro="The three this comparison gets asked most, answered in a couple of sentences each."
    items={FAQ}
  />

  <ArticleFooter lastReviewed={LAST_REVIEWED} />
</Base>

<style>
  .bp-vs-head {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }
  .bp-vs-head__h1 {
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5.6vw, 3.1rem);
    line-height: 1.06;
    letter-spacing: -0.024em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    background: linear-gradient(172deg, #ffffff 4%, #e4e9f1 44%, #a2aab8 100%);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }
  .bp-vs-head__stand {
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2.4vw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  .bp-vs-head__hero {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 14px);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bp-vs-head__hero img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
